provider_responses.text:
The most important point is this:

**A photo can make normal facial asymmetry look much larger than it really is.**  
Before concluding that one side of your face is structurally different, first separate **real anatomy** from **camera/lighting distortion**.

Why this matters:

- A close phone selfie uses a wide-angle lens. The side closer to the camera can look wider, more projected, or more prominent.
- If your head is turned even slightly, one eye, cheek, jaw, or nostril can appear larger.
- Light from one side creates deeper shadows under one eye, cheekbone, nose, or jaw. The shadow can be interpreted as a hollow, droop, or uneven bone structure.
- Phone image processing often adds local contrast and sharpening, exaggerating pores, folds, under-eye shadows, and facial contour differences.
- Faces are naturally asymmetric anyway: one brow may sit a little higher, one eyelid may open differently, one cheek may be fuller, and one side of the mouth may move more when smiling.

So the correct way to assess it is not: “Does this one photo look asymmetric?”

It is: **“Does the same difference remain visible in several properly controlled photos, in different lighting, with my face and camera centered?”**

A likely real, stable asymmetry:
- repeats in front-facing, evenly lit photos;
- remains visible at normal camera distance;
- stays in the same location;
- is present in mirrors as well as photographs;
- does not depend strongly on expression, head position, or light direction.

A likely photographic/optical effect:
- changes sides or severity between photos;
- is strongest in close selfies;
- disappears in diffuse light;
- appears mostly as a shadow/contour difference;
- looks different when you rotate your head a few degrees or change camera height.

And importantly, **small stable asymmetry is normal**, not evidence that something is wrong. The more medically important category is not mild unevenness—it is a **new, progressive, or functional change**. For example: sudden one-sided facial weakness, a newly dropping mouth corner, inability to close one eye, numbness, severe pain/swelling, or a rapid change in facial shape. Those are different from ordinary lifelong asymmetry and should be assessed promptly.

